    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">1. How the 45-Degree Wedge Creates Holding Power</h2>
      <p>
        A French cleat works using simple mechanical wedging. A wall-mounted cleat is ripped with a 45-degree upward-facing bevel. The matching hanger attached to your tool caddy has a downward-facing 45-degree bevel. Gravity pulls the tool caddy downward, which drives the two opposing 45-degree wedges together, clamping the tool tighter against the wall as weight increases.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">2. Weight Capacity: Where Does Failure Occur?</h2>
      <p>
        In physical load testing, French cleats almost never fail at the 45-degree wood wedge itself. Failure occurs in one of two places:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Drywall Crushing:</strong> If the cleat is screwed into drywall anchors without penetrating wall studs, the gypsum core crumbles under as little as 25 to 40 pounds.</li>
        <li><strong>Screw Fastener Shear:</strong> Standard drywall screws are brittle and will snap under shear loads. You must use <strong>#8 or #9 structural wood screws (such as GRK R4 or Spax Cabinet Screws)</strong> embedded at least 1.5 inches into 2x4 solid wood wall studs.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-amber-600 p-4 rounded-r-lg font-mono text-sm text-gray-800">
        <p>Total Safe Working Load = (Number of Stud Fasteners) &times; 75 lbs per screw</p>
        <p class="mt-1 text-xs text-gray-600">Example: An 8-foot cleat spanning across 6 studs (16" o.c.) with 2 screws per stud = 12 screws &times; 75 lbs = <strong>900 lbs safe capacity</strong>.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">3. Plywood Selection: 3/4" vs 1/2"</h2>
      <p>
        Always use <strong>3/4-inch (18mm) Baltic birch or cabinet-grade hardwood plywood</strong>. Never use MDF or particleboard for wall cleats; composite core materials lack long-grain tensile strength and will delaminate along the 45-degree bevel under cyclic shock loads.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">4. Safe Cutting Geometry on a Table Saw</h2>
      <p>
        To get clean, matching bevels from a single 4x8 plywood sheet:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Rip strips 5 inches wide with your blade set at 90 degrees.</li>
        <li>Tilt your table saw blade to exactly 45 degrees.</li>
        <li>Set the fence to cut precisely in the center of the strip, yielding two interlocking 2-1/2" wide cleats from every rip.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

